## How it works

{/* TODO: Explain the tick model in prose. Verified mechanics, in order:
     1. The sidecar detects the end of the customer's turn — a final transcription result
        followed by a brief silence (idle_timeout_ms), or the agent starting to speak.
     2. It sends the running transcript to the model with your prompt and your SWAIG tools.
     3. The model returns one line of agent-facing advice (an `insight`), or calls the
        built-in `sidecar_skip` tool to stay silent.
     4. Any tools it calls run through your SWAIG functions and MCP servers.
     5. Every step is reported as a structured callback carrying a `tick_id`.
     Keep this tight — the reference documents the full callback catalog. */}

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Caller["Caller"]
    Agent["Your human agent"]
    Sidecar["AI Sidecar<br/>listens only"]
    App["Your application<br/>agent's screen"]

    Caller <-->|"the call"| Agent
    Caller -.->|"transcript"| Sidecar
    Agent -.->|"transcript"| Sidecar
    Sidecar -->|"insight callbacks"| App
    App -->|"rendered advice"| Agent
```

{/* TODO(verify): the diagram asserts the sidecar hears both legs. That matches the
     "both legs required" limitation in the reference, but confirm on a real call. */}

## Before you start

{/* TODO: The prerequisites that actually bite:
     - A call with two legs. A single-leg direction is rejected outright.
     - A publicly reachable webhook URL — the sidecar's whole output arrives there,
       so a localhost URL means a sidecar that appears to do nothing.
     - Somewhere to render the advice. Without a UI consuming the callbacks there is
       nothing to see, which is the most common "it didn't work" report.
     Three bullets, or a short paragraph. Link setup, don't re-teach it. */}

## Attach a sidecar

{/* TODO: The main path. The natural shape is a `connect`-bridged call — the customer talks
     to the human, the sidecar coaches the human's screen.
     Minimum viable config is prompt + lang + a webhook URL; add one SWAIG function so the
     example does real work (house rule: no knowledge-trivia bots).
     VALIDATE against a real call before shipping. */}

<CodeBlocks>
<CodeBlock title="YAML">
```yaml
version: 1.0.0
sections:
  main:
    - answer: {}
    - ai_sidecar:
        lang: en-US
        prompt: |
          # TODO: the operator prompt that coaches the dispatcher.
          # Tell it to call sidecar_skip when there is nothing useful to say.
        # TODO: webhook URL and SWAIG functions — see the reference for exact keys.
    - connect:
        to: # TODO: the human agent
```
</CodeBlock>
<CodeBlock title="JSON">
```json
{
  "version": "1.0.0",
