## Write the prompt

{/* TODO: The prompt is what separates a useful sidecar from a noisy one, so this section
     earns its length. Verified points worth making:
     - The prompt is the operator prompt: it describes coaching behavior, not conversation.
       SignalWire adds the built-in role instructions, so you don't restate them.
     - Advice is one line per tick. Write for a glance, not a paragraph.
     - Instruct the model to call `sidecar_skip` when there is nothing useful to say.
       Without that instruction it fills silence with low-quality advice. This is the
       highest-value sentence on the page.
     - `sidecar_skip` is reserved — never define a function with that name.
     Link [prompt-engineering] for general technique rather than repeating it. */}

## Receive the output

{/* TODO: Everything the sidecar produces arrives as webhook callbacks — insights, skips,
     tool activity, stop and error events, each carrying a `tick_id` that ties it to one turn.
     Show one insight payload and say which fields a UI actually renders.
     Full catalog is in the reference; the payload shape is in [sidecar-callback].
     General webhook handling: [webhooks]. */}

## Give it tools

{/* TODO: SWAIG functions work the same way they do for a voice agent — link [tool-calling]
     rather than re-teaching the round trip. What is worth covering HERE is what differs:
     - `say` does not speak. The sidecar has no voice, so a `say` action is reported as a
       callback instead of being spoken aloud. Readers coming from the `ai` method assume
       otherwise, so state it.
     - Tool loops are capped: too many calls in one tick, a repeated call, or an unregistered
       tool stops tool calling for that tick and returns an `error` callback with
       error_reason: tool_loop. Tell readers the fix is clearer tool results and a prompt
       that says what to do after a tool returns.
     Tool webhook contract: [sidecar-tool-webhook]. */}

## Limitations

{/* TODO: These are verified from the reference and all of them cost real debugging time:
     - One transcriber per call. `ai_sidecar` and [live-transcribe] cannot run on the same
       call at once; starting one while the other is active is rejected.
     - Both legs required. A single-leg direction is rejected.
     - Turn detection is tuned for Deepgram, the default speech engine.
     - `say` is reported, never spoken.
     - The sidecar stops with the call — a `final` callback fires on hangup.
     Prose or a list, but keep the cause-and-symptom pairing. */}

{/* TODO(cost): a second model runs for the length of the call. Add a sentence on the
     billing and latency implication once confirmed. [NEEDS SOURCE: sidecar pricing] */}

## Confirm it works

{/* TODO: The observable proof, and for this feature it is subtler than usual — a sidecar
     that is working correctly may legitimately stay silent.
     Give the reader:
     1. the first callback they should see, and roughly when;
     2. a line the caller can say that should reliably produce an insight;
     3. how to tell "working and correctly quiet" from "not running at all"
        (a `skip` callback proves the tick fired; silence proves nothing).
     Pair it with the likeliest failure: no callbacks at all almost always means the
     webhook URL is unreachable. */}

